VALUES (
  '2026/10/12/canada-pauses-for-thanksgiving-job-losses-inflation-rising-yields-bank-of-canada',
  'economy', 'article',
  'Canada Pauses for Thanksgiving With Job Losses, 3.0% Inflation and Rising Yields Facing the Bank of Canada', 'September job losses, 3.0% inflation and a 3.99% 10-year yield frame the October 28 decision, on a day Canada pauses to give thanks.',
  '<ul>
<li><strong>Canada lost 68,300 jobs in September,</strong><span> lifting unemployment to 6.5% as the labour force kept shrinking.</span></li>
<li><strong>August CPI held at 3.0%,</strong><span> while CPI-trim at 1.9% and CPI-median at 2.0% sit near the 2% target.</span></li>
<li><strong>The Bank of Canada decides October 28</strong><span> at 2.25%, after saying upside inflation risks had increased.</span></li>
<li><strong>The 10-year Government of Canada yield reached 3.99%,</strong><span> against 3.45% at the start of July, lifting fixed mortgage rates.</span></li>
<li><strong>Parliament fixed Thanksgiving on January 31, 1957,</strong><span> as the second Monday in October.</span></li>
</ul>',
  '<p>Canada lost 68,300 jobs in September, and the unemployment rate rose to 6.5% from 6.4%, Statistics Canada reported Friday. The decline follows a 41,700 loss in August, and the rate stayed near its two-year low largely because the labour force kept shrinking. The Bank of Canada makes its next decision on October 28, 16 days from now, with the policy rate at 2.25%.</p>
<p>Inflation is pulling the other way. Headline CPI held at 3.0% in August, with gasoline doing most of the work: excluding gasoline, prices rose 2.4%, and the Bank of Canada core gauges, CPI-trim at 1.9% and CPI-median at 2.0%, sit at or just below the 2% target.</p>
<p>A 10-cent-per-litre federal gasoline tax holiday, extended through year-end, is already holding down pump prices. InvestingLive noted that gasoline has still risen materially in September, which makes the September CPI release on October 19 the last major data point before the decision.</p>
<h2>Why October 28 Is Harder Than the Headline Numbers Suggest</h2>
<p>The Bank of Canada held at 2.25% on September 2 and said upside risks to inflation had increased, adding that it was prepared to adjust policy as needed, according to the Canadian Real Estate Association summary of the decision. That language put a rate increase back on the table. A contracting labour market complicates any move to deliver one.</p>
<p>Trade policy is the swing factor. The United States imposed 50% tariffs on C$27.6 billion of Canadian goods on August 22, roughly 5% of exports, and Canada answered with matching counter-tariffs on September 8 after negotiations collapsed, according to Reuters and the Government of Canada.</p>
<p>ATB Economics estimates the combined measures will reduce Canadian GDP growth by roughly half a percentage point, with the largest effect in 2027, and expects the Bank to hold through 2026. The central bank therefore faces a shrinking workforce, core inflation near target and a tariff shock that raises prices while it slows growth. The October 28 announcement arrives with an updated Monetary Policy Report.</p>
<h2>The Bond Market Is Setting Mortgage Costs Before the Bank Does</h2>
<p>The Government of Canada five-year yield has climbed to about 3.7%, more than three times the sub-1% levels that prevailed when millions of Canadians took out five-year fixed mortgages in 2020 and 2021, according to Canadian Mortgage Professional magazine. The 10-year yield rose to 3.99% from 3.45% at the start of July, and the two-year moved from 2.76% to 3.37% over the same period, according to Morningstar.</p>
<p>That repricing reaches households without any Bank of Canada move. The lowest insured five-year fixed mortgage rate was 4.34% on October 7, according to Ratehub.ca, and Equifax Canada reported that mortgage delinquency balances rose 32% year over year nationally in the first quarter of 2026, and 52% in Ontario.</p>
<p>The S&amp;P/TSX Composite has fallen 3.5% from its August 25 close of 36,957.63 over a period in which tariffs, a firmer central bank tone and higher yields arrived in sequence, then rebounded 1.48% on Friday to close at 35,664.62.</p>
