<p style="font-size:11px;color:#666;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;margin-top:6px;">The decline from the August 25 close spans the U.S. Section 338 tariffs of August 22 and the Canadian counter-tariffs of September 8. Friday gained 519.24 points, or 1.48%, after the September labour force survey.</p>
<p>Crude traded near US$91.67 a barrel and gold near US$4,208 an ounce in late-morning trading Friday, according to The Canadian Press, with the Canadian dollar near 70 cents U.S. The Toronto Stock Exchange is closed today for Thanksgiving and reopens Tuesday, October 13, according to TMX Group.</p>
<h2>A Short History of Canadian Thanksgiving</h2>
<p>Canadian Thanksgiving is older than most of the institutions that now observe it. In 1578, the English explorer Martin Frobisher and his crew held a ceremony of thanks in the eastern Arctic after a difficult voyage, which the Canadian Encyclopedia credits as the first of its kind held by Europeans in North America. In the early 1600s, Samuel de Champlain founded the Order of Good Cheer at Port Royal, a series of rotating feasts shared with Mi’kmaq neighbours that helped settlers through the winter.</p>
<p>The date moved for centuries. Parliament declared November 6, 1879 a national day of thanks, and the holiday later shared a date with Armistice Day until Remembrance Day was set apart in 1931. On January 31, 1957, Parliament fixed the observance on the second Monday in October, tied to the harvest that the holiday was created to mark.</p>
<h2>What the Day Means for Financial Advisors</h2>
<p>This Thanksgiving falls between a labour report showing 68,300 lost jobs and a rate decision 16 days away, a calendar that tests client relationships more than portfolios. Across Canada, advisors will spend part of the weekend with the families whose finances they know in detail: the couple renewing a mortgage signed in 2021, the retiree watching a withdrawal schedule, the business owner pricing tariff costs into next year.</p>
<p>Trust in the profession is built in unremarkable moments: a call returned during a 3.5% drawdown, a renewal discussed six months early, a plan that stated in advance what a pullback of this size would mean.</p>
<p>Gratitude, in that setting, is practical. It is the recognition that a family has handed over decisions about its security, and that the handover carries an obligation the markets do not pause for, even on a holiday Monday.</p>
<h2>A Note of Thanks</h2>
<p>To the advisors and readers of HDQ across Canada: thank you for the attention you give this publication each morning, and for the work done for the families who rely on you. We hope the long weekend leaves room for a proper meal and an unhurried conversation. Happy Thanksgiving from everyone at HDQ Editorial.</p>
<p>Markets reopen Tuesday, and HDQ publishes in full that morning.</p>',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What They''re Feeling</div>
<p>Clients with mortgages renewing in the next year are anxious, because a five-year yield near 3.7% has pushed the lowest insured fixed rate to 4.34%. Clients close to retirement are unsettled by a headline showing 68,300 lost jobs. Clients with Canadian equity exposure feel the 3.5% pullback from August 25 more than Friday''s 1.5% rebound, and the long weekend has given them time to read the news with no one to ask about it.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">What to Say</div>
<div class="script-box">Thank you for making time on a holiday weekend. I want to put three numbers in context. Canada lost 68,300 jobs in September, inflation is 3.0% mostly because of gasoline, and the Bank of Canada decides on October 28 with those two numbers pulling in opposite directions. The TSX is about 3.5% below its late-August high, a pullback of a size our plan already allows for. The number that matters most for households is not the Bank of Canada rate. It is the five-year Government of Canada bond yield, now about 3.7%, because it sets fixed mortgage rates. If your mortgage renews in the next 12 months, let us review your options well before the offer arrives. I do not see a reason to change your plan because of last week alone, and I will tell you if that changes. I will call you after the October 28 decision to walk through what the Bank says.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Who''s Affected</div>
<p><strong>High impact:</strong> households renewing five-year fixed mortgages signed in 2020 or 2021, business owners in sectors hit by U.S. tariffs (wine, furniture, dairy, cement, clothing) or by Canadian counter-tariffs on inputs, and Ontario homeowners given the 52% rise in mortgage delinquency balances.</p>
<p><strong>Mixed impact:</strong> balanced portfolios, where a 3.5% equity pullback and higher yields both affect valuations, and clients near retirement who depend on both employment income and portfolio withdrawals.</p>
<p><strong>Potential benefit:</strong> clients reinvesting maturing GICs or short-term bonds at a two-year yield of 3.37%, and clients with energy exposure while crude trades near US$91 a barrel.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Action Checklist</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Pull every client with a mortgage renewal in the next 12 months and rank by renewal date.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Compare fixed income duration against the 10-year yield of 3.99% and note any positions that moved with it.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Flag business-owner clients in tariff-exposed sectors for a cash flow conversation.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Calendar the September CPI release on October 19 and the Bank of Canada decision on October 28.</div>
<div class="checklist-item">Document each client conversation and the guidance given.</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Follow-Up Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="respond-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> Three numbers from the long weekend<br><br>
Hi [Client Name],<br><br>
Thank you for speaking with me. As promised, here are the three numbers we discussed. Canada lost 68,300 jobs in September. Inflation was 3.0% in August, mostly because of gasoline. The five-year Government of Canada bond yield is about 3.7%, which is what sets fixed mortgage rates.<br><br>
The Bank of Canada announces its next decision on October 28. I will contact you afterward to walk through what it means for your plan. If your mortgage renews in the next 12 months, I would like to review the timing with you before an offer arrives.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''respond-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>
',
  '<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Client Profiles to Target</div>
<p><strong>DIY investors with a mortgage renewing in the next 12 months:</strong> they follow the Bank of Canada rate but often not the bond yield that sets fixed rates, and they have no one to model a 4.34% renewal against a 2021 rate.</p>
<p><strong>Business owners in tariff-exposed sectors:</strong> U.S. tariffs on wine, furniture, dairy, cement and clothing, and Canadian counter-tariffs on inputs, raise cash flow questions with no easy owner inside the company.</p>
<p><strong>Recent retirees holding cash or short-term GICs:</strong> a two-year yield of 3.37% changes the reinvestment decision, and no advisor is looking at it with them.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Opening Line</div>
<div class="script-box">I am calling because the five-year Government of Canada yield has moved up to about 3.7% since July, and I am speaking with people whose mortgages renew in the next year about what that does to their payment. Does yours?</div>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Value Proposition</div>
<p>Last week produced a contracting labour market and a rising yield at the same time, an unusual pairing that leaves self-directed households without a framework. A household tracking the Bank of Canada rate sees 2.25%, while the lowest insured five-year fixed rate has reached 4.34%.</p>
<p>That gap is the opening. An advisor can model a renewal against a 2020 or 2021 starting rate, test the cash flow and sequence the decisions, while a self-directed household faces the same choice with only the lender renewal letter as a guide.</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Discovery Questions</div>
<p>1. When does your mortgage renew, and have you received an offer?</p>
<p>2. Are you leaning toward a fixed term or staying variable, and what is driving that?</p>
<p>3. How much of a higher monthly payment could your cash flow absorb before something else has to change?</p>
<p>4. If you needed to draw on investments to cover an increase, which accounts would you use, and what would the tax cost be?</p>
<p>5. Who do you talk to about decisions like this today?</p>
</div>
<div class="toolkit-section">
<div class="toolkit-section-label">Prospecting Email Template</div>
<div class="email-box" id="prospect-email">
<strong>Subject:</strong> The number behind your mortgage renewal<br><br>
Hi [Prospect Name],<br><br>
The five-year Government of Canada bond yield has risen to about 3.7%, up sharply since July, and the lowest insured five-year fixed mortgage rate reached 4.34% on October 7. For households renewing a mortgage signed in 2020 or 2021, that changes the payment.<br><br>
I help clients model a renewal against their full cash flow before the offer arrives. If it would be useful, I can walk you through yours in 20 minutes.<br><br>
[Your Name]<br><br>
<em>This communication is for educational purposes only and does not constitute personalized investment advice.</em>
</div>
<button class="btn-copy" onclick="copyEmail(''prospect-email'', this)">Copy email</button>
</div>
